## Primitives in the hydra.core.lib.regex module.
##
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual: they are specified once, in Hydra's regular-expression specification, and the same pattern means the same thing on every host.
## Matching is leftmost-longest: a match begins at the earliest possible position in the string, and among the matches beginning at that position the longest is chosen.
## findAll, replaceAll, and split operate on non-overlapping matches, found left to right.
module hydra.core.lib.regex

import hydra.core.paths
import hydra.core.ast
import hydra.core.coders
import hydra.core.model
import hydra.core.diff
import hydra.core.docs
import hydra.core.error.checking
import hydra.core.error.model
import hydra.core.error.file
import hydra.core.error.packaging
import hydra.core.error.system
import hydra.core.errors
import hydra.core.file
import hydra.core.graph
import hydra.core.json.model
import hydra.core.markdown
import hydra.core.packaging
import hydra.core.parsing
import hydra.core.query
import hydra.core.regex
import hydra.core.relational
import hydra.core.system
import hydra.core.tabular
import hydra.core.testing
import hydra.core.time
import hydra.core.topology
import hydra.core.typed
import hydra.core.typing
import hydra.core.util
import hydra.core.validation
import hydra.core.variants

## Find the first regex match within a string, returning the matched substring if any.
##
## find(pat, s) returns Just(t) where t is the leftmost-longest substring of s matching pat, or Nothing if pat does not match anywhere in s.
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual; see docs/specification/regex.md.
## Total; a pattern that does not parse under the Hydra regex grammar is treated as not matching anywhere, the same as a well-formed pattern with no match.
##
## @availableSince 0.15
## @requirement returnsLeftmostLongestMatch find returns the leftmost-longest substring matching the pattern, wrapped, when the pattern matches anywhere in the string, including alternation where the longer branch wins and greedy quantifiers that pick the longest run.
## @requirement noneWhenNoMatch find returns none when the pattern does not match anywhere in the string, including on the empty string, and when the pattern is ill-formed under the Hydra regex grammar (treated the same as a well-formed pattern with no match, never a host-specific parse error).
primitive find : ∀.string → string → optional<string>

## Find all non-overlapping regex matches within a string.
##
## findAll(pat, s) returns the list of all leftmost-longest, non-overlapping matches of pat in s, in the order they appear. Returns the empty list if pat does not match anywhere.
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual; see docs/specification/regex.md.
## Total; an unparseable pattern yields the empty list, the same as a well-formed pattern with no matches.
##
## @availableSince 0.15
## @requirement returnsAllNonOverlappingMatchesInOrder findAll returns every leftmost-longest, non-overlapping match of the pattern in the string, in the order they appear.
## @requirement emptyListWhenNoMatch findAll returns the empty list when the pattern does not match anywhere, including when the pattern is ill-formed under the Hydra regex grammar (treated the same as a well-formed pattern with no matches).
primitive findAll : ∀.string → string → list<string>

## Test whether a regex matches an entire string.
##
## matches(pat, s) returns true iff pat matches the whole of s (anchored at both ends; a match of a proper substring of s does not suffice). To test whether pat matches anywhere within s, use find.
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual; see docs/specification/regex.md.
## Total; an unparseable pattern yields false, the same as a well-formed pattern with no match.
##
## @availableSince 0.15
## @requirement isWholeStringAnchored matches is always anchored to the whole string at both ends, regardless of explicit ^/$ in the pattern: a match of a proper substring does not suffice, and ^/$ never match at an internal line break (string-boundary anchors, not line-boundary).
## @requirement testsMinimalCoreGrammar matches correctly evaluates every construct in the minimal-core regex grammar: literals, character classes (including negation and ranges), the wildcard (matching any character including newline), quantifiers (?, *, +, {n}, {n,}, {n,m}), alternation (leftmost-longest preference, not leftmost-first), and non-capturing grouping (including nested and quantified groups).
## @requirement treatsEscapedMetacharactersAsLiterals matches treats a backslash-escaped metacharacter as the literal character, both at top level and inside a character class, and treats an unescaped dash as a literal at top level (never forming a range outside a class) while forming a range only inside a class.
## @requirement falseWhenNoMatchOrIllFormed matches returns false when the pattern does not match the whole string, including when the pattern is ill-formed under the Hydra regex grammar (treated the same as a well-formed pattern with no match, never a host-specific parse error) and for constructs outside the minimal core (such as the {,m} quantifier form).
primitive matches : ∀.string → string → boolean

## Replace the first regex match in a string with a replacement string.
##
## replace(pat, repl, s) returns s with the first leftmost-longest match of pat replaced by repl. If pat does not match, s is returned unchanged.
## Replacement-string syntax (capture-group references such as $1 or \\1, literal escapes) is host-defined.
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual; see docs/specification/regex.md.
## Total; an unparseable pattern leaves s unchanged, the same as a well-formed pattern with no match.
##
## @availableSince 0.15
## @requirement replacesLeftmostLongestMatch replace replaces the first (leftmost-longest) match of the pattern with the replacement string, including an anchored match at the start of the string.
## @requirement unchangedWhenNoMatch replace returns the string unchanged when the pattern does not match anywhere, including when the pattern is ill-formed under the Hydra regex grammar (treated the same as a well-formed pattern with no match).
primitive replace : ∀.string → string → string → string

## Replace all non-overlapping regex matches in a string with a replacement string.
##
## replaceAll(pat, repl, s) returns s with every leftmost-longest, non-overlapping match of pat replaced by repl. If pat does not match anywhere, s is returned unchanged.
## Replacement-string syntax is host-defined; see replace for the caveat.
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual; see docs/specification/regex.md.
## Total; an unparseable pattern leaves s unchanged, the same as a well-formed pattern with no match.
##
## @availableSince 0.15
## @requirement replacesEveryNonOverlappingMatch replaceAll replaces every leftmost-longest, non-overlapping match of the pattern with the replacement string, including when the replacement string is empty.
## @requirement unchangedWhenNoMatch replaceAll returns the string unchanged when the pattern does not match anywhere, including when the pattern is ill-formed under the Hydra regex grammar (treated the same as a well-formed pattern with no match).
primitive replaceAll : ∀.string → string → string → string

## Split a string by occurrences of a regex pattern.
##
## split(pat, s) returns the list of substrings of s obtained by splitting on every leftmost-longest, non-overlapping match of pat.
## Trailing empty splits are host-defined (some engines retain them, some discard them; for portable code, do not rely on the trailing-empty behavior).
## Pattern syntax and semantics are Hydra-defined and translingual; see docs/specification/regex.md.
## Total; an unparseable pattern yields the single-element list containing s, the same as a well-formed pattern with no match.
##
## @availableSince 0.15
## @requirement splitsOnEveryNonOverlappingMatch split returns the substrings of the string obtained by splitting on every leftmost-longest, non-overlapping match of the pattern, including splitting on a fixed delimiter, on a character class, and splitting into a trailing empty substring when the pattern matches at the end of the string.
## @requirement singleElementListWhenNoMatchOrIllFormed split returns the single-element list containing the original string unchanged when the pattern does not match anywhere, including when the pattern is ill-formed under the Hydra regex grammar (treated the same as a well-formed pattern with no match).
primitive split : ∀.string → string → list<string>